window.FERN_DATA = window.FERN_DATA || {};
FERN_DATA["terminal"] = {"units": [{"id": "term-start", "title": "Getting started", "blurb": "What the terminal is, how a command is built, and how to get help.", "topics": [{"id": "what-is-terminal", "title": "Terminal, shell and zsh", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Terminal is the app with the text window. The shell is the program inside it that reads what you type and runs it. On your Mac, that shell is zsh.", "deep": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Terminal:</strong> the macOS app (Applications → Utilities → Terminal, or ⌘-Space and type \"Terminal\"). IntelliJ also has one built in (View → Tool Windows → Terminal).</li>\n<li><strong>Shell:</strong> the program that reads your commands and runs them. macOS uses <strong>zsh</strong> (\"Z shell\"). Linux machines usually use <strong>bash</strong>, which is nearly identical for everything on this page.</li>\n<li><strong>Command line / CLI:</strong> typing commands instead of clicking.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why bother?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some tools only exist on the command line (Git, SSH, Homebrew, most cybersecurity tools).</li>\n<li>One command can do what would take 50 clicks.</li>\n<li>Commands can be saved as scripts and rerun.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The prompt</strong> is the text before your cursor. It usually shows your user, computer and current folder:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>noah@MacBook-Air ~ %\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><code>~</code> means you're in your home folder.</li>\n<li><code>%</code> is zsh's prompt symbol. Linux bash shows <code>$</code>. In guides, <strong>don't type</strong> the <code>%</code> or <code>$</code>. It just marks \"type this\".</li>\n</ul>", "text": "terminal: the macos app (applications → utilities → terminal, or ⌘-space and type \"terminal\"). intellij also has one built in (view → tool windows → terminal). shell: the program that reads your commands and runs them. macos uses zsh (\"z shell\"). linux machines usually use bash , which is nearly identical for everything on this page. command line / cli: typing commands instead of clicking. why bother? some tools only exist on the command line (git, ssh, homebrew, most cybersecurity tools). one command can do what would take 50 clicks. commands can be saved as scripts and rerun. the prompt is the text before your cursor. it usually shows your user, computer and current folder: noah@macbook-air ~ % ~ means you're in your home folder. % is zsh's prompt symbol. linux bash shows $ . in guides, don't type the % or $ . it just marks \"type this\"."}, {"id": "command-anatomy", "title": "How a command is built", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Every command has the same shape: the command name, then options that change how it behaves (they start with a dash), then arguments that say what to act on. Spaces separate the parts.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ls<span class=\"w\">  </span>-l<span class=\"w\">  </span>Documents\n<span class=\"c1\">#│   │      └── argument: what to act on</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">#│   └── option (flag): how to do it</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">#└── command: what to do</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Options</strong> usually start with <code>-</code> (short: <code>-l</code>) or <code>--</code> (long: <code>--help</code>).</li>\n<li>Short options can be combined: <code>ls -l -a</code> is the same as <code>ls -la</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spaces matter.</strong> <code>cd Documents</code> works, <code>cdDocuments</code> doesn't.</li>\n<li><strong>Case matters.</strong> <code>Documents</code> and <code>documents</code> are different names.</li>\n<li>Press <strong>Return</strong> to run. Nothing happens until you do.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>No news is good news:</strong> many commands print nothing when they succeed. If you see no error, it worked.</p>", "text": "ls -l documents #│ │ └── argument: what to act on #│ └── option (flag): how to do it #└── command: what to do options usually start with - (short: -l ) or -- (long: --help ). short options can be combined: ls -l -a is the same as ls -la . spaces matter. cd documents works, cddocuments doesn't. case matters. documents and documents are different names. press return to run. nothing happens until you do. no news is good news: many commands print nothing when they succeed. if you see no error, it worked."}, {"id": "getting-help", "title": "Getting help", "src": "Fern", "idea": "You don't need to memorize options. man shows a command's full manual, and --help gives a quick summary for many tools.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>man<span class=\"w\"> </span>ls<span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"c1\"># full manual for ls</span>\nls<span class=\"w\"> </span>--help<span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># quick summary (works for many, not all, commands)</span>\nwhich<span class=\"w\"> </span>java<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># where a command's program lives</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># what kind of command it is</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Inside <code>man</code>:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Key</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Space / b</td>\n<td>page down / up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/word</code> then Return</td>\n<td>search for \"word\" (n = next match)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>q</td>\n<td>quit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> <code>brew install tldr</code>, then <code>tldr tar</code> shows short, practical examples instead of a long manual. Also try pasting a confusing command into explainshell.com to see each part explained.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<details><summary>Check yourself: how do you get out of a man page?</summary><p>Press q.</p></details>", "text": "man ls # full manual for ls ls --help # quick summary (works for many, not all, commands) which java # where a command's program lives type cd # what kind of command it is inside man : key does space / b page down / up /word then return search for \"word\" (n = next match) q quit tip: brew install tldr , then tldr tar shows short, practical examples instead of a long manual. also try pasting a confusing command into explainshell.com to see each part explained. check yourself: how do you get out of a man page? press q."}, {"id": "shortcuts", "title": "Keyboard shortcuts that save time", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Tab finishes names for you, the up arrow brings back old commands, and Control-C stops whatever is running. Those three alone make the terminal much faster.", "deep": "<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Keys</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tab</strong></td>\n<td>autocomplete a file or folder name (press twice to see options)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↑ / ↓</strong></td>\n<td>previous / next command from history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Control-C</strong></td>\n<td>stop the running command (your emergency brake)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Control-L</strong> or <code>clear</code></td>\n<td>clear the screen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Control-A / Control-E</strong></td>\n<td>jump to start / end of the line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Control-U</strong></td>\n<td>delete the whole line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Control-R</strong></td>\n<td>search your command history as you type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Option-click</strong></td>\n<td>move the cursor to where you click (Mac Terminal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>⌘-T</strong></td>\n<td>new tab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>⌘-K</strong></td>\n<td>clear everything (Mac Terminal)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code>history</code> lists your past commands. <code>!!</code> reruns the last one.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Heads up:</strong> Control-C does <strong>not</strong> copy in the terminal. Use ⌘-C to copy, ⌘-V to paste.</p>\n</blockquote>", "text": "keys does tab autocomplete a file or folder name (press twice to see options) ↑ / ↓ previous / next command from history control-c stop the running command (your emergency brake) control-l or clear clear the screen control-a / control-e jump to start / end of the line control-u delete the whole line control-r search your command history as you type option-click move the cursor to where you click (mac terminal) ⌘-t new tab ⌘-k clear everything (mac terminal) history lists your past commands. !! reruns the last one. heads up: control-c does not copy in the terminal. use ⌘-c to copy, ⌘-v to paste."}, {"id": "terminal-safety", "title": "Staying safe", "src": "Fern", "idea": "The terminal does exactly what you say, right away, with no undo and no trash can. Read commands before you run them, especially anything with rm, sudo, or something pasted from the internet.", "deep": "<ul>\n<li><strong>No Trash:</strong> files deleted with <code>rm</code> are gone for good.</li>\n<li><strong><code>sudo</code></strong> runs a command as administrator (\"superuser do\"). It asks for your Mac password, and nothing appears as you type it. That's normal. Only use <code>sudo</code> when you understand why it's needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Pasted commands:</strong> never paste a command you don't understand, especially ones with <code>sudo</code>, <code>rm -rf</code>, or <code>curl ... | sh</code>. Look it up first.</li>\n<li><strong>Stuck?</strong> Control-C stops most things. Closing the window stops everything in it.</li>\n<li><strong>Practice somewhere safe:</strong> make a folder like <code>~/terminal-practice</code> and experiment inside it.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>mkdir<span class=\"w\"> </span>~/terminal-practice\n<span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>~/terminal-practice\n</code></pre></div>", "text": "no trash: files deleted with rm are gone for good. sudo runs a command as administrator (\"superuser do\"). it asks for your mac password, and nothing appears as you type it. that's normal. only use sudo when you understand why it's needed. pasted commands: never paste a command you don't understand, especially ones with sudo , rm -rf , or curl ... | sh . look it up first. stuck? control-c stops most things. closing the window stops everything in it. practice somewhere safe: make a folder like ~/terminal-practice and experiment inside it. mkdir ~/terminal-practice cd ~/terminal-practice"}]}, {"id": "term-navigate", "title": "Moving around", "blurb": "The file system, paths, and getting from folder to folder.", "topics": [{"id": "paths", "title": "The file system and paths", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Your files form a tree that starts at / (the root). A path is the route to a file. Absolute paths start from / or ~; relative paths start from wherever you are now.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>/                         root of the whole disk\n├── Applications\n└── Users\n    └── noahhoagland      ← your home folder, written ~\n        ├── Desktop\n        │   └── cs study\n        ├── Documents\n        └── Downloads\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol</th>\n<th>Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/</code></td>\n<td>the root (top of the tree) and the separator between folders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>~</code></td>\n<td>your home folder (<code>/Users/noahhoagland</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.</code></td>\n<td>the folder you're in now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>..</code></td>\n<td>the folder one level up (the parent)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Absolute vs relative</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>/Users/noahhoagland/Desktop<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\"># absolute: starts at /</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>~/Desktop<span class=\"w\">                     </span><span class=\"c1\"># absolute: starts at home</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>Desktop<span class=\"w\">                       </span><span class=\"c1\"># relative: only works if you're in ~</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>../Documents<span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"c1\"># relative: up one level, then into Documents</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Spaces in names:</strong> the shell splits on spaces, so quote the path or escape the space.</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"cs study\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>cs<span class=\"se\">\\ </span>study\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> drag a file or folder from Finder into the Terminal window and its full path is typed for you.</p>\n</blockquote>", "text": "/ root of the whole disk ├── applications └── users └── noahhoagland ← your home folder, written ~ ├── desktop │ └── cs study ├── documents └── downloads symbol means / the root (top of the tree) and the separator between folders ~ your home folder ( /users/noahhoagland ) . the folder you're in now .. the folder one level up (the parent) absolute vs relative cd /users/noahhoagland/desktop # absolute: starts at / cd ~/desktop # absolute: starts at home cd desktop # relative: only works if you're in ~ cd ../documents # relative: up one level, then into documents spaces in names: the shell splits on spaces, so quote the path or escape the space. cd \"cs study\" cd cs \\ study tip: drag a file or folder from finder into the terminal window and its full path is typed for you."}, {"id": "pwd-cd-ls", "title": "pwd, cd and ls", "src": "Fern", "idea": "pwd tells you where you are, cd moves you somewhere else, and ls lists what's in a folder. Most terminal sessions start with these three.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">pwd</span><span class=\"w\">                 </span><span class=\"c1\"># print working directory: where am I?</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>Desktop<span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"c1\"># change directory</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>..<span class=\"w\">               </span><span class=\"c1\"># go up one level</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"c1\"># go home (same as cd ~)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>-<span class=\"w\">                </span><span class=\"c1\"># go back to the previous folder</span>\nls<span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"c1\"># list this folder</span>\nls<span class=\"w\"> </span>Desktop<span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"c1\"># list another folder</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Useful <code>ls</code> options</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ls -l</code></td>\n<td>long format: permissions, size, date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ls -a</code></td>\n<td>all, including hidden files (names starting with <code>.</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ls -la</code></td>\n<td>both</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ls -lh</code></td>\n<td>sizes in KB/MB instead of bytes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ls -lt</code></td>\n<td>newest first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ls -R</code></td>\n<td>everything inside subfolders too</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Hidden files</strong> start with a dot, like <code>.zshrc</code> and <code>.git</code>. They're usually settings.</p>\n<p><strong>Mac extra:</strong> <code>open .</code> opens the current folder in Finder. <code>open file.pdf</code> opens a file in its default app.</p>\n<details><summary>Check yourself: you're in ~/Desktop/cs study/java study. What does cd ../.. do?</summary><p>Goes up two levels, to ~/Desktop.</p></details>", "text": "pwd # print working directory: where am i? cd desktop # change directory cd .. # go up one level cd # go home (same as cd ~) cd - # go back to the previous folder ls # list this folder ls desktop # list another folder useful ls options command shows ls -l long format: permissions, size, date ls -a all, including hidden files (names starting with . ) ls -la both ls -lh sizes in kb/mb instead of bytes ls -lt newest first ls -r everything inside subfolders too hidden files start with a dot, like .zshrc and .git . they're usually settings. mac extra: open . opens the current folder in finder. open file.pdf opens a file in its default app. check yourself: you're in ~/desktop/cs study/java study. what does cd ../.. do? goes up two levels, to ~/desktop."}, {"id": "tree-practice", "title": "Practice: find your way around", "src": "Fern", "idea": "A 10-minute drill: move through your own folders using only the terminal, checking where you are as you go.", "deep": "<ol>\n<li>Open Terminal. Run <code>pwd</code>. You should be in <code>/Users/noahhoagland</code>.</li>\n<li><code>cd Desktop</code>, then <code>ls</code>. Do you see <code>cs study</code>?</li>\n<li><code>cd \"cs study\"</code> (or type <code>cd cs</code> and press Tab).</li>\n<li><code>ls -la</code>. Spot any hidden files?</li>\n<li><code>cd ..</code> then <code>pwd</code>. Back on Desktop?</li>\n<li><code>cd -</code>. Where did that take you?</li>\n<li><code>cd</code> on its own, then <code>pwd</code>. Home again.</li>\n<li><code>open .</code> to see the same folder in Finder.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> <code>brew install tree</code>, then <code>tree -L 2</code> draws the folder structure two levels deep.</p>\n</blockquote>", "text": "open terminal. run pwd . you should be in /users/noahhoagland . cd desktop , then ls . do you see cs study ? cd \"cs study\" (or type cd cs and press tab). ls -la . spot any hidden files? cd .. then pwd . back on desktop? cd - . where did that take you? cd on its own, then pwd . home again. open . to see the same folder in finder. tip: brew install tree , then tree -l 2 draws the folder structure two levels deep."}]}, {"id": "term-files", "title": "Working with files", "blurb": "Creating, copying, moving, deleting, reading and editing files.", "topics": [{"id": "make-files", "title": "Making files and folders", "src": "Fern", "idea": "mkdir makes a folder and touch makes an empty file. Add -p to mkdir to create a whole chain of folders at once.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>mkdir<span class=\"w\"> </span>projects<span class=\"w\">                    </span><span class=\"c1\"># one folder</span>\nmkdir<span class=\"w\"> </span>-p<span class=\"w\"> </span>projects/java/hello<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># nested folders, all at once</span>\ntouch<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\">                   </span><span class=\"c1\"># empty file (or updates its date if it exists)</span>\ntouch<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java<span class=\"w\"> </span>Helper.java<span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># several files</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Avoid spaces in names you'll use in the terminal. <code>cs-study</code> or <code>cs_study</code> saves quoting.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "mkdir projects # one folder mkdir -p projects/java/hello # nested folders, all at once touch notes.txt # empty file (or updates its date if it exists) touch main.java helper.java # several files avoid spaces in names you'll use in the terminal. cs-study or cs_study saves quoting."}, {"id": "copy-move-delete", "title": "Copy, move, rename and delete", "src": "Fern", "idea": "cp copies, mv moves or renames, and rm deletes forever. Add -r to work on whole folders.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>cp<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes-backup.txt<span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># copy a file</span>\ncp<span class=\"w\"> </span>-r<span class=\"w\"> </span>projects<span class=\"w\"> </span>projects-backup<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># copy a folder (-r = recursive)</span>\nmv<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\"> </span>Documents/<span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># move into a folder</span>\nmv<span class=\"w\"> </span>old-name.txt<span class=\"w\"> </span>new-name.txt<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\"># rename (a move to a new name)</span>\nrm<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes-backup.txt<span class=\"w\">                 </span><span class=\"c1\"># delete a file</span>\nrm<span class=\"w\"> </span>-r<span class=\"w\"> </span>projects-backup<span class=\"w\">               </span><span class=\"c1\"># delete a folder and everything in it</span>\nrmdir<span class=\"w\"> </span>empty-folder<span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"c1\"># delete an empty folder</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-r</code></td>\n<td>recursive: include everything inside folders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-i</code></td>\n<td>interactive: ask before each overwrite or delete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-v</code></td>\n<td>verbose: print what it's doing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Heads up:</strong> <code>cp</code> and <code>mv</code> silently overwrite a file with the same name. <code>rm</code> skips the Trash. When unsure, use <code>-i</code>: <code>rm -i file.txt</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Wildcards</strong> match many names at once:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ls<span class=\"w\"> </span>*.java<span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"c1\"># every file ending in .java</span>\nrm<span class=\"w\"> </span>*.class<span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># delete every compiled class file in this folder</span>\ncp<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.*<span class=\"w\"> </span>backup/<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\"># Main.java, Main.class, ...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p>Run <code>ls</code> with a wildcard first to see what it matches, <strong>then</strong> use it with <code>rm</code>.</p>", "text": "cp notes.txt notes-backup.txt # copy a file cp -r projects projects-backup # copy a folder (-r = recursive) mv notes.txt documents/ # move into a folder mv old-name.txt new-name.txt # rename (a move to a new name) rm notes-backup.txt # delete a file rm -r projects-backup # delete a folder and everything in it rmdir empty-folder # delete an empty folder option means -r recursive: include everything inside folders -i interactive: ask before each overwrite or delete -v verbose: print what it's doing heads up: cp and mv silently overwrite a file with the same name. rm skips the trash. when unsure, use -i : rm -i file.txt . wildcards match many names at once: ls *.java # every file ending in .java rm *.class # delete every compiled class file in this folder cp main.* backup/ # main.java, main.class, ... run ls with a wildcard first to see what it matches, then use it with rm ."}, {"id": "view-files", "title": "Reading files", "src": "Fern", "idea": "cat prints a whole file, less lets you scroll through a long one, and head and tail show just the start or end.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>cat<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"c1\"># print the whole file</span>\nless<span class=\"w\"> </span>long-log.txt<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># scroll (Space, b, /search, q to quit)</span>\nhead<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># first 10 lines</span>\nhead<span class=\"w\"> </span>-n<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\"># first 3 lines</span>\ntail<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># last 10 lines</span>\ntail<span class=\"w\"> </span>-f<span class=\"w\"> </span>app.log<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\"># keep watching as new lines arrive (Control-C to stop)</span>\nwc<span class=\"w\"> </span>-l<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\"># count lines</span>\n</code></pre></div>", "text": "cat notes.txt # print the whole file less long-log.txt # scroll (space, b, /search, q to quit) head notes.txt # first 10 lines head -n 3 notes.txt # first 3 lines tail notes.txt # last 10 lines tail -f app.log # keep watching as new lines arrive (control-c to stop) wc -l notes.txt # count lines"}, {"id": "edit-files", "title": "Editing files", "src": "Fern", "idea": "nano is a simple text editor that runs inside the terminal. The shortcuts are listed at the bottom of its screen; ^ means the Control key.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>nano<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># open (or create) the file in nano</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Keys</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>type normally</td>\n<td>edit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control-O, then Return</td>\n<td>save (\"write Out\")</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control-X</td>\n<td>exit (asks to save if needed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control-W</td>\n<td>search</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control-K / Control-U</td>\n<td>cut line / paste</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Other ways to edit</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>open<span class=\"w\"> </span>-e<span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># open in TextEdit</span>\nidea<span class=\"w\"> </span>.<span class=\"w\">                 </span><span class=\"c1\"># open this folder in IntelliJ (if its command-line launcher is set up)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> you'll see <code>vim</code> in tutorials. If you land in it by accident: press Esc, type <code>:q!</code>, press Return to quit without saving.</p>\n</blockquote>", "text": "nano notes.txt # open (or create) the file in nano keys does type normally edit control-o, then return save (\"write out\") control-x exit (asks to save if needed) control-w search control-k / control-u cut line / paste other ways to edit open -e notes.txt # open in textedit idea . # open this folder in intellij (if its command-line launcher is set up) tip: you'll see vim in tutorials. if you land in it by accident: press esc, type :q! , press return to quit without saving."}, {"id": "pipes-search", "title": "Pipes, redirects and searching", "src": "Fern", "idea": "> saves a command's output to a file, and | (a pipe) feeds one command's output into another. grep finds lines containing text, and find finds files by name.", "deep": "<p><strong>Redirects</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ls<span class=\"w\"> </span>&gt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>files.txt<span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># write output to a file (replaces it)</span>\nls<span class=\"w\"> </span>&gt;&gt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>files.txt<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\"># add to the end of the file</span>\njavac<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">2</span>&gt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>errors.txt<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\"># save error output</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Pipes:</strong> chain small commands together.</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ls<span class=\"w\"> </span>-l<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>less<span class=\"w\">                 </span><span class=\"c1\"># scroll through a long listing</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">history</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>grep<span class=\"w\"> </span>javac<span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># past commands that mention javac</span>\ncat<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>wc<span class=\"w\"> </span>-l<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\"># count lines of code</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>grep: search inside files</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>grep<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"public class\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>*.java<span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># lines containing the text</span>\ngrep<span class=\"w\"> </span>-i<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"todo\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>*.java<span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"c1\"># ignore case</span>\ngrep<span class=\"w\"> </span>-rn<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Scanner\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>.<span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># search all subfolders, show line numbers</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>find: search for files by name</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>find<span class=\"w\"> </span>.<span class=\"w\"> </span>-name<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"*.java\"</span><span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"c1\"># every .java file below here</span>\nfind<span class=\"w\"> </span>~/Desktop<span class=\"w\"> </span>-name<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"*Study*\"</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\"># by part of a name</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Run one command after another</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;&amp;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>java<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"c1\"># second runs only if the first succeeded</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<details><summary>Check yourself: what's the difference between > and >>?</summary><p>&gt; replaces the file's contents. &gt;&gt; adds to the end and keeps what was there.</p></details>", "text": "redirects ls > files.txt # write output to a file (replaces it) ls >> files.txt # add to the end of the file javac main.java 2 > errors.txt # save error output pipes: chain small commands together. ls -l | less # scroll through a long listing history | grep javac # past commands that mention javac cat main.java | wc -l # count lines of code grep: search inside files grep \"public class\" *.java # lines containing the text grep -i \"todo\" *.java # ignore case grep -rn \"scanner\" . # search all subfolders, show line numbers find: search for files by name find . -name \"*.java\" # every .java file below here find ~/desktop -name \"*study*\" # by part of a name run one command after another javac main.java && java main # second runs only if the first succeeded check yourself: what's the difference between > and >>? > replaces the file's contents. >> adds to the end and keeps what was there."}]}, {"id": "term-java", "title": "Running Java from the terminal", "blurb": "Compiling and running your programs without IntelliJ, from one file up to packages, JARs and JavaFX.", "topics": [{"id": "java-check", "title": "Check which Java you have", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Before compiling, make sure the terminal can find a JDK and that it's the version you expect. java -version and javac -version tell you.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>java<span class=\"w\"> </span>-version\njavac<span class=\"w\"> </span>-version\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>openjdk version \"21.0.4\" 2024-07-16\njavac 21.0.4\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>If you see <code>command not found</code> or the wrong version</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>/usr/libexec/java_home<span class=\"w\"> </span>-V<span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"c1\"># list every JDK installed on this Mac</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>JDKs that IntelliJ downloaded live in <code>~/Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines</code> and show up in that list, but the terminal doesn't use them automatically.</li>\n<li>Point the terminal at Java 21 by adding this line to <code>~/.zshrc</code> (see <em>Your shell setup</em>):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">JAVA_HOME</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"k\">$(</span>/usr/libexec/java_home<span class=\"w\"> </span>-v<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">21</span><span class=\"k\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">PATH</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"nv\">$JAVA_HOME</span><span class=\"s2\">/bin:</span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>No JDK at all? Install one with Homebrew (see <em>Installing a JDK with Homebrew</em>).</li>\n</ul>", "text": "java -version javac -version openjdk version \"21.0.4\" 2024-07-16 javac 21.0.4 if you see command not found or the wrong version /usr/libexec/java_home -v # list every jdk installed on this mac jdks that intellij downloaded live in ~/library/java/javavirtualmachines and show up in that list, but the terminal doesn't use them automatically. point the terminal at java 21 by adding this line to ~/.zshrc (see your shell setup ): export java_home = $( /usr/libexec/java_home -v 21 ) export path = \" $java_home /bin: $path \" no jdk at all? install one with homebrew (see installing a jdk with homebrew )."}, {"id": "java-compile-run", "title": "Compile and run one file", "src": "Fern", "idea": "javac turns Main.java into Main.class (bytecode), then java Main runs it. Run both from the folder that holds the file, and leave .class off the run command.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>~/Desktop/hello\njavac<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\"># creates Main.class</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\">              </span><span class=\"c1\"># runs it (no .class!)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Shortcut for a single file</strong> (Java 11+): compile and run in one step, with no .class file left behind.</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>java<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Passing arguments</strong> (they arrive in <code>main</code>'s <code>String[] args</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>java<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\"> </span>Noah<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">3</span><span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># args[0] = \"Noah\", args[1] = \"3\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Do it all on one line:</strong> <code>javac Main.java &amp;&amp; java Main</code>. The <code>&amp;&amp;</code> means \"run only if compiling worked\".</p>\n<p><strong>Reading compiler errors</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>Main.java:7: error: ';' expected\n        System.out.println(\"Hi\")\n                                ^\n1 error\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p>File name, <strong>line number</strong>, the problem, and a <code>^</code> pointing at the spot. Fix the first error first; later ones are often caused by it.</p>\n<details><summary>Check yourself: java Main.class gives an error. Why?</summary><p>The java command takes a class name, not a file name. Use java Main.</p></details>", "text": "cd ~/desktop/hello javac main.java # creates main.class java main # runs it (no .class!) shortcut for a single file (java 11+): compile and run in one step, with no .class file left behind. java main.java passing arguments (they arrive in main 's string[] args ): java main noah 3 # args[0] = \"noah\", args[1] = \"3\" do it all on one line: javac main.java && java main . the && means \"run only if compiling worked\". reading compiler errors main.java:7: error: ';' expected system.out.println(\"hi\") ^ 1 error file name, line number , the problem, and a ^ pointing at the spot. fix the first error first; later ones are often caused by it. check yourself: java main.class gives an error. why? the java command takes a class name, not a file name. use java main."}, {"id": "java-input-files", "title": "Feeding input from a file", "src": "Fern", "idea": "The < redirect sends a file's contents into your program as if you typed them. That makes testing Scanner programs fast and repeatable.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>java<span class=\"w\"> </span>GradeCalculator<span class=\"w\"> </span>&lt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>test-input.txt\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>GradeCalculator<span class=\"w\"> </span>&lt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>test-input.txt<span class=\"w\"> </span>&gt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>output.txt<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\"># save the output too</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><code>test-input.txt</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>93\n78\n85\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Each line answers one <code>nextInt()</code> / <code>nextLine()</code> call.</li>\n<li>Keep a few input files (normal, edge case, bad input) and rerun them after each change. That's a lightweight version of the test cases from Software Engineering.</li>\n<li><code>diff output.txt expected.txt</code> shows any difference between two files. No output means they match.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "java gradecalculator < test-input.txt java gradecalculator < test-input.txt > output.txt # save the output too test-input.txt : 93 78 85 each line answers one nextint() / nextline() call. keep a few input files (normal, edge case, bad input) and rerun them after each change. that's a lightweight version of the test cases from software engineering. diff output.txt expected.txt shows any difference between two files. no output means they match."}, {"id": "java-multi-file", "title": "Several files, an output folder and the classpath", "src": "Fern", "idea": "For projects with more than one class, compile them all together and put the .class files in their own folder with -d. Then tell java where to find them with -cp (the classpath).", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>*.java<span class=\"w\">                 </span><span class=\"c1\"># compile every .java file here</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\">                    </span><span class=\"c1\"># run the class that has main</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Keep source and compiled files apart</strong> (cleaner, and easy to ignore in Git):</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>project/\n├── src/   Main.java  Student.java\n└── out/   (compiled .class files go here)\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>-d<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>src/*.java<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># -d: where to put .class files</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>-cp<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"c1\"># -cp: where to look for classes</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Packages:</strong> the folder path must match the <code>package</code> line, and you run the class by its full name.</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>src/com/noah/grades/Main.java     →  package com.noah.grades;\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>-d<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">$(</span>find<span class=\"w\"> </span>src<span class=\"w\"> </span>-name<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"*.java\"</span><span class=\"k\">)</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>-cp<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>com.noah.grades.Main\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Using a library .jar</strong>: list it on the classpath, separated by <code>:</code> on Mac and Linux.</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>-cp<span class=\"w\"> </span>lib/gson.jar<span class=\"w\"> </span>-d<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>src/*.java\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>-cp<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"out:lib/gson.jar\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>Main\n</code></pre></div>", "text": "javac *.java # compile every .java file here java main # run the class that has main keep source and compiled files apart (cleaner, and easy to ignore in git): project/ ├── src/ main.java student.java └── out/ (compiled .class files go here) javac -d out src/*.java # -d: where to put .class files java -cp out main # -cp: where to look for classes packages: the folder path must match the package line, and you run the class by its full name. src/com/noah/grades/main.java → package com.noah.grades; javac -d out $( find src -name \"*.java\" ) java -cp out com.noah.grades.main using a library .jar : list it on the classpath, separated by : on mac and linux. javac -cp lib/gson.jar -d out src/*.java java -cp \"out:lib/gson.jar\" main"}, {"id": "java-jar", "title": "Packaging a runnable JAR", "src": "Fern", "idea": "A JAR bundles your compiled classes into one file. Give it a main class and anyone with Java can run it with java -jar.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>-d<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>src/*.java\njar<span class=\"w\"> </span>--create<span class=\"w\"> </span>--file<span class=\"w\"> </span>grades.jar<span class=\"w\"> </span>--main-class<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\"> </span>-C<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>.\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>-jar<span class=\"w\"> </span>grades.jar\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><code>-C out .</code> means \"go into <code>out</code> and include everything there\".</li>\n<li><code>jar --list --file grades.jar</code> shows what's inside.</li>\n<li>A JAR is a good thing to attach to a portfolio project release.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "javac -d out src/*.java jar --create --file grades.jar --main-class main -c out . java -jar grades.jar -c out . means \"go into out and include everything there\". jar --list --file grades.jar shows what's inside. a jar is a good thing to attach to a portfolio project release."}, {"id": "java-javafx", "title": "Running JavaFX from the terminal", "src": "Fern, Java Ch. 14", "idea": "JavaFX isn't part of the JDK, so you have to tell both javac and java where the JavaFX SDK's lib folder is and which modules to load.", "deep": "<p>Save the path once, in <code>~/.zshrc</code>, pointing at the <code>lib</code> folder of your downloaded SDK:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">PATH_TO_FX</span><span class=\"o\">=</span>~/javafx-sdk-21.0.10/lib\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p>Then compile and run:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>--module-path<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH_TO_FX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>--add-modules<span class=\"w\"> </span>javafx.controls<span class=\"w\"> </span>HelloFX.java\njava<span class=\"w\">  </span>--module-path<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH_TO_FX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>--add-modules<span class=\"w\"> </span>javafx.controls<span class=\"w\"> </span>HelloFX\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Add more modules with commas: <code>--add-modules javafx.controls,javafx.fxml,javafx.media</code>.</li>\n<li>The error <code>Module javafx.controls not found</code> means the path is wrong. Check it with <code>ls $PATH_TO_FX</code>.</li>\n<li>This is the same <code>--module-path</code> setting IntelliJ needs in its VM options.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "save the path once, in ~/.zshrc , pointing at the lib folder of your downloaded sdk: export path_to_fx = ~/javafx-sdk-21.0.10/lib then compile and run: javac --module-path $path_to_fx --add-modules javafx.controls hellofx.java java --module-path $path_to_fx --add-modules javafx.controls hellofx add more modules with commas: --add-modules javafx.controls,javafx.fxml,javafx.media . the error module javafx.controls not found means the path is wrong. check it with ls $path_to_fx . this is the same --module-path setting intellij needs in its vm options."}]}, {"id": "term-brew", "title": "Homebrew", "blurb": "The Mac's package manager: one command to install, update and remove developer tools.", "topics": [{"id": "brew-what", "title": "What Homebrew is and installing it", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Homebrew is a package manager: instead of downloading installers from websites, you type brew install and the tool is downloaded, set up and kept updatable.", "deep": "<p><strong>Install</strong> (paste the command from the official site, brew.sh):</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>/bin/bash<span class=\"w\"> </span>-c<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"k\">$(</span>curl<span class=\"w\"> </span>-fsSL<span class=\"w\"> </span>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh<span class=\"k\">)</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>It asks for your Mac password and may install Apple's Command Line Tools first (that also gives you <code>git</code>). This takes a few minutes.</li>\n<li>This is the one time pasting a <code>curl ... | bash</code>-style command is OK: it comes straight from brew.sh, the official source.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Finish setup on Apple Silicon (your M4):</strong> at the end, the installer prints <strong>\"Next steps\"</strong> with commands that add Homebrew to your PATH. Run them. They look like this:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s1\">'eval \"$(/opt/homebrew/bin/brew shellenv)\"'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>&gt;&gt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>~/.zprofile\n<span class=\"nb\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"k\">$(</span>/opt/homebrew/bin/brew<span class=\"w\"> </span>shellenv<span class=\"k\">)</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Check it worked</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>brew<span class=\"w\"> </span>--version\nbrew<span class=\"w\"> </span>doctor<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\"># \"Your system is ready to brew.\" means all good</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>On Apple Silicon Macs, Homebrew lives in <code>/opt/homebrew</code>.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "install (paste the command from the official site, brew.sh): /bin/bash -c \" $( curl -fssl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/homebrew/install/head/install.sh ) \" it asks for your mac password and may install apple's command line tools first (that also gives you git ). this takes a few minutes. this is the one time pasting a curl ... | bash -style command is ok: it comes straight from brew.sh, the official source. finish setup on apple silicon (your m4): at the end, the installer prints \"next steps\" with commands that add homebrew to your path. run them. they look like this: echo 'eval \"$(/opt/homebrew/bin/brew shellenv)\"' >> ~/.zprofile eval \" $( /opt/homebrew/bin/brew shellenv ) \" check it worked brew --version brew doctor # \"your system is ready to brew.\" means all good on apple silicon macs, homebrew lives in /opt/homebrew ."}, {"id": "brew-use", "title": "Everyday Homebrew commands", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Search for a tool, install it, and keep everything updated. Formulae are command-line tools; casks are regular Mac apps.", "deep": "<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew search name</code></td>\n<td>find a package</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew info name</code></td>\n<td>details about it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew install name</code></td>\n<td>install a command-line tool (a <strong>formula</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew install --cask name</code></td>\n<td>install a Mac app (a <strong>cask</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew list</code></td>\n<td>what you've installed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew update</code></td>\n<td>refresh Homebrew's list of packages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew upgrade</code></td>\n<td>upgrade everything you installed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew outdated</code></td>\n<td>what has updates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew uninstall name</code></td>\n<td>remove it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew cleanup</code></td>\n<td>delete old versions to free space</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew doctor</code></td>\n<td>diagnose problems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Good first installs</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>brew<span class=\"w\"> </span>install<span class=\"w\"> </span>tree<span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># draws folder trees</span>\nbrew<span class=\"w\"> </span>install<span class=\"w\"> </span>tldr<span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># short example-based help pages</span>\nbrew<span class=\"w\"> </span>install<span class=\"w\"> </span>git<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\"># newer Git than Apple's</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Weekly habit:</strong> <code>brew update &amp;&amp; brew upgrade</code></p>", "text": "command does brew search name find a package brew info name details about it brew install name install a command-line tool (a formula ) brew install --cask name install a mac app (a cask ) brew list what you've installed brew update refresh homebrew's list of packages brew upgrade upgrade everything you installed brew outdated what has updates brew uninstall name remove it brew cleanup delete old versions to free space brew doctor diagnose problems good first installs brew install tree # draws folder trees brew install tldr # short example-based help pages brew install git # newer git than apple's weekly habit: brew update && brew upgrade"}, {"id": "brew-java", "title": "Installing a JDK with Homebrew", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Homebrew can install OpenJDK 21. One extra link step lets macOS's own Java tools find it.", "deep": "<p>Check first: if <code>java -version</code> already says 21, skip this.</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>brew<span class=\"w\"> </span>install<span class=\"w\"> </span>openjdk@21\nsudo<span class=\"w\"> </span>ln<span class=\"w\"> </span>-sfn<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">$(</span>brew<span class=\"w\"> </span>--prefix<span class=\"k\">)</span>/opt/openjdk@21/libexec/openjdk.jdk<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"se\">\\</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span>/Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines/openjdk-21.jdk\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>The <code>\\</code> at the end of a line continues the command on the next line.</li>\n<li><code>brew info openjdk@21</code> prints the exact link command for your machine; use that if it differs.</li>\n<li>Then verify: <code>/usr/libexec/java_home -V</code> and <code>java -version</code>.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "check first: if java -version already says 21, skip this. brew install openjdk@21 sudo ln -sfn $( brew --prefix ) /opt/openjdk@21/libexec/openjdk.jdk \\ /library/java/javavirtualmachines/openjdk-21.jdk the \\ at the end of a line continues the command on the next line. brew info openjdk@21 prints the exact link command for your machine; use that if it differs. then verify: /usr/libexec/java_home -v and java -version ."}]}, {"id": "term-setup", "title": "Your shell setup", "blurb": "Settings files, environment variables, PATH, aliases and permissions.", "topics": [{"id": "zshrc", "title": "The .zshrc file", "src": "Fern", "idea": "~/.zshrc is a hidden settings file that zsh runs every time you open a new terminal window. Put your aliases, PATH changes and exports there so they stick.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>nano<span class=\"w\"> </span>~/.zshrc<span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"c1\"># edit it</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">source</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>~/.zshrc<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\"># reload it in the current window</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Changes only apply to <strong>new</strong> windows, or after <code>source</code>.</li>\n<li><code>~/.zprofile</code> is similar but runs once at login. Homebrew's setup line goes there.</li>\n<li>Back it up before big edits: <code>cp ~/.zshrc ~/.zshrc.backup</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A starter <code>.zshrc</code></strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\"># Java</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">JAVA_HOME</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"k\">$(</span>/usr/libexec/java_home<span class=\"w\"> </span>-v<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">21</span><span class=\"k\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">PATH</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"nv\">$JAVA_HOME</span><span class=\"s2\">/bin:</span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">PATH_TO_FX</span><span class=\"o\">=</span>~/javafx-sdk-21.0.10/lib\n\n<span class=\"c1\"># Shortcuts</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">alias</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">ll</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"ls -lah\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">alias</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>..<span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"cd ..\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">alias</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">cs</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"cd ~/Desktop/'cs study'\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>", "text": "nano ~/.zshrc # edit it source ~/.zshrc # reload it in the current window changes only apply to new windows, or after source . ~/.zprofile is similar but runs once at login. homebrew's setup line goes there. back it up before big edits: cp ~/.zshrc ~/.zshrc.backup . a starter .zshrc # java export java_home = $( /usr/libexec/java_home -v 21 ) export path = \" $java_home /bin: $path \" export path_to_fx = ~/javafx-sdk-21.0.10/lib # shortcuts alias ll = \"ls -lah\" alias .. = \"cd ..\" alias cs = \"cd ~/desktop/'cs study'\""}, {"id": "env-path", "title": "Environment variables and PATH", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Environment variables are named settings the shell keeps, like HOME. PATH is the important one: a list of folders the shell searches when you type a command.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$HOME</span><span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># /Users/noahhoagland</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH</span><span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># folders separated by :</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">GREETING</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"hi\"</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\"># set one for this window (put it in .zshrc to keep it)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$GREETING</span>\nenv<span class=\"w\">                    </span><span class=\"c1\"># list them all</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>How PATH works:</strong> when you type <code>java</code>, zsh checks each folder in PATH, in order, for a program named <code>java</code>, and runs the first one it finds.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>command not found</code> means no folder in PATH has it: it isn't installed, or its folder isn't in PATH.</li>\n<li><code>which java</code> shows which one wins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Adding a folder to PATH</strong> (in <code>~/.zshrc</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">PATH</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"nv\">$HOME</span><span class=\"s2\">/bin:</span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"c1\"># new folder first, then everything that was there</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Heads up:</strong> always include <code>:$PATH</code>. Writing <code>export PATH=\"$HOME/bin\"</code> alone replaces the whole list, and commands like <code>ls</code> will \"disappear\" until you open a new window.</p>\n</blockquote>", "text": "echo $home # /users/noahhoagland echo $path # folders separated by : export greeting = \"hi\" # set one for this window (put it in .zshrc to keep it) echo $greeting env # list them all how path works: when you type java , zsh checks each folder in path, in order, for a program named java , and runs the first one it finds. command not found means no folder in path has it: it isn't installed, or its folder isn't in path. which java shows which one wins. adding a folder to path (in ~/.zshrc ): export path = \" $home /bin: $path \" # new folder first, then everything that was there heads up: always include :$path . writing export path=\"$home/bin\" alone replaces the whole list, and commands like ls will \"disappear\" until you open a new window."}, {"id": "aliases", "title": "Aliases", "src": "Fern", "idea": "An alias is your own short name for a longer command. Define it in .zshrc and it's available in every new window.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">alias</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">ll</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"ls -lah\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">alias</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">jc</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"javac -d out src/*.java\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">alias</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">jr</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"java -cp out Main\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">alias</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">gs</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"git status\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>No spaces around <code>=</code>.</li>\n<li><code>alias</code> on its own lists all your aliases. <code>unalias ll</code> removes one.</li>\n<li>Keep them few and memorable. When an alias grows beyond one line, make it a script or function instead.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "alias ll = \"ls -lah\" alias jc = \"javac -d out src/*.java\" alias jr = \"java -cp out main\" alias gs = \"git status\" no spaces around = . alias on its own lists all your aliases. unalias ll removes one. keep them few and memorable. when an alias grows beyond one line, make it a script or function instead."}, {"id": "permissions", "title": "File permissions and chmod", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Every file has read, write and execute permissions for three groups: you (the owner), your group, and everyone else. chmod changes them; the most common use is chmod +x to make a script runnable.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ls<span class=\"w\"> </span>-l<span class=\"w\"> </span>run.sh\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>-rwxr-xr--  1 noahhoagland  staff  120 Oct 21 14:00 run.sh\n│└┬┘└┬┘└┬┘\n│ │  │  └── others: r-- (read only)\n│ │  └───── group:  r-x (read, execute)\n│ └──────── owner:  rwx (read, write, execute)\n└────────── type: - file, d folder\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Letter</th>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>r</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>w</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>write</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>execute (run a file / enter a folder)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>chmod<span class=\"w\"> </span>+x<span class=\"w\"> </span>run.sh<span class=\"w\">                 </span><span class=\"c1\"># let it run as a program</span>\nchmod<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">644</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>notes.txt<span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># rw- r-- r--  (normal file)</span>\nchmod<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">755</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>run.sh<span class=\"w\">                </span><span class=\"c1\"># rwx r-x r-x  (normal script)</span>\nchmod<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">600</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>~/.ssh/id_ed25519<span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"c1\"># rw- --- ---  (private key: only you)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Add the numbers per group: 7 = 4+2+1 (rwx), 5 = 4+1 (r-x), 6 = 4+2 (rw-).</li>\n<li><code>Permission denied</code> usually means a missing <code>x</code> (scripts) or not owning the file.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "ls -l run.sh -rwxr-xr-- 1 noahhoagland staff 120 oct 21 14:00 run.sh │└┬┘└┬┘└┬┘ │ │ │ └── others: r-- (read only) │ │ └───── group: r-x (read, execute) │ └──────── owner: rwx (read, write, execute) └────────── type: - file, d folder letter number means r 4 read w 2 write x 1 execute (run a file / enter a folder) chmod +x run.sh # let it run as a program chmod 644 notes.txt # rw- r-- r-- (normal file) chmod 755 run.sh # rwx r-x r-x (normal script) chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 # rw- --- --- (private key: only you) add the numbers per group: 7 = 4+2+1 (rwx), 5 = 4+1 (r-x), 6 = 4+2 (rw-). permission denied usually means a missing x (scripts) or not owning the file."}]}, {"id": "term-ssh", "title": "SSH basics", "blurb": "Securely logging into other computers and copying files between them.", "topics": [{"id": "ssh-what", "title": "What SSH is and connecting", "src": "Fern", "idea": "SSH (Secure Shell) gives you an encrypted terminal on another computer. You type commands on your Mac, and they run on the remote machine until you type exit.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ssh<span class=\"w\"> </span>username@hostname<span class=\"w\">              </span><span class=\"c1\"># e.g. ssh noah@192.168.1.20</span>\nssh<span class=\"w\"> </span>-p<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">2222</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>username@hostname<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># a non-standard port (default is 22)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">exit</span><span class=\"w\">                               </span><span class=\"c1\"># leave the remote machine</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>The first time you connect</strong> you'll see something like:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>The authenticity of host 'example.com' can't be established.\nED25519 key fingerprint is SHA256:abc123...\nAre you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no)?\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Type <code>yes</code>. The server's fingerprint is saved in <code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code>.</li>\n<li>If you later get a <strong>\"REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED\"</strong> warning, stop. Either the server was rebuilt or something is intercepting the connection. Check with whoever runs the server before continuing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Watch the prompt:</strong> after connecting, it shows the remote machine's name. Commands now run <em>there</em>, usually on Linux with bash.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Ethics:</strong> only SSH into machines you own, lab environments built for practice, or systems you have written permission to use. Your cybersecurity course says the same.</p>\n</blockquote>", "text": "ssh username@hostname # e.g. ssh noah@192.168.1.20 ssh -p 2222 username@hostname # a non-standard port (default is 22) exit # leave the remote machine the first time you connect you'll see something like: the authenticity of host 'example.com' can't be established. ed25519 key fingerprint is sha256:abc123... are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no)? type yes . the server's fingerprint is saved in ~/.ssh/known_hosts . if you later get a \"remote host identification has changed\" warning, stop. either the server was rebuilt or something is intercepting the connection. check with whoever runs the server before continuing. watch the prompt: after connecting, it shows the remote machine's name. commands now run there , usually on linux with bash. ethics: only ssh into machines you own, lab environments built for practice, or systems you have written permission to use. your cybersecurity course says the same."}, {"id": "ssh-keys", "title": "SSH keys", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Instead of a password, SSH can use a key pair: a private key that never leaves your Mac and a public key you give to servers. The server checks you hold the matching private key.", "deep": "<p><strong>Make a key pair</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ssh-keygen<span class=\"w\"> </span>-t<span class=\"w\"> </span>ed25519<span class=\"w\"> </span>-C<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"your_email@example.com\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Press Return to accept the default location (<code>~/.ssh/id_ed25519</code>).</li>\n<li>Set a <strong>passphrase</strong>. It protects the key if your laptop is ever stolen.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>File</th>\n<th>What</th>\n<th>Share it?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>~/.ssh/id_ed25519</code></td>\n<td>private key</td>\n<td><strong>Never</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub</code></td>\n<td>public key</td>\n<td>Yes, give it to servers and GitHub</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Put your public key on a server</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ssh-copy-id<span class=\"w\"> </span>username@hostname\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p>After that, <code>ssh username@hostname</code> logs in with your key.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember the passphrase in the Mac keychain</strong> so you don't retype it:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ssh-add<span class=\"w\"> </span>--apple-use-keychain<span class=\"w\"> </span>~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n</code></pre></div>", "text": "make a key pair ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -c \"your_email@example.com\" press return to accept the default location ( ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 ). set a passphrase . it protects the key if your laptop is ever stolen. file what share it? ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 private key never ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub public key yes, give it to servers and github put your public key on a server ssh-copy-id username@hostname after that, ssh username@hostname logs in with your key. remember the passphrase in the mac keychain so you don't retype it: ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/id_ed25519"}, {"id": "ssh-config", "title": "The SSH config file and copying files", "src": "Fern", "idea": "~/.ssh/config lets you give servers short nicknames. scp copies files to and from a remote machine using the same login.", "deep": "<p><strong><code>~/.ssh/config</code></strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>Host *\n  AddKeysToAgent yes\n  UseKeychain yes\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n\nHost lab\n  HostName 192.168.1.20\n  User noah\n  Port 22\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p>Now <code>ssh lab</code> does the whole login.</p>\n<p><strong>Copy files with <code>scp</code></strong> (remote paths are <code>host:path</code>)</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>scp<span class=\"w\"> </span>report.txt<span class=\"w\"> </span>lab:~/<span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"c1\"># Mac → remote home folder</span>\nscp<span class=\"w\"> </span>lab:~/results.txt<span class=\"w\"> </span>.<span class=\"w\">                </span><span class=\"c1\"># remote → current folder here</span>\nscp<span class=\"w\"> </span>-r<span class=\"w\"> </span>project<span class=\"w\"> </span>lab:~/projects/<span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># a whole folder</span>\n</code></pre></div>", "text": "~/.ssh/config host * addkeystoagent yes usekeychain yes identityfile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 host lab hostname 192.168.1.20 user noah port 22 now ssh lab does the whole login. copy files with scp (remote paths are host:path ) scp report.txt lab:~/ # mac → remote home folder scp lab:~/results.txt . # remote → current folder here scp -r project lab:~/projects/ # a whole folder"}, {"id": "ssh-github", "title": "SSH with GitHub", "src": "Fern", "idea": "GitHub uses SSH keys so Git can push and pull without a password. Add your public key to your GitHub account, then test the connection.", "deep": "<ol>\n<li>Copy your public key: <code>pbcopy &lt; ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub</code></li>\n<li>On GitHub: <strong>Settings → SSH and GPG keys → New SSH key</strong>, paste, save.</li>\n<li>Test:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ssh<span class=\"w\"> </span>-T<span class=\"w\"> </span>git@github.com\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>Hi noah! You've successfully authenticated, but GitHub does not provide shell access.\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Clone with the SSH address: <code>git clone git@github.com:user/repo.git</code></li>\n</ol>", "text": "copy your public key: pbcopy < ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub on github: settings → ssh and gpg keys → new ssh key , paste, save. test: ssh -t git@github.com hi noah! you've successfully authenticated, but github does not provide shell access. clone with the ssh address: git clone git@github.com:user/repo.git"}, {"id": "ssh-practice", "title": "Practice SSH safely: OverTheWire Bandit", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Bandit is a free, legal game built for practicing the terminal over SSH. Each level's password is hidden somewhere on the server, and finding it teaches you a command.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ssh<span class=\"w\"> </span>bandit0@bandit.labs.overthewire.org<span class=\"w\"> </span>-p<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">2220</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># password for level 0: bandit0</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Each level's page tells you the goal and suggests commands to look up.</li>\n<li>It exercises everything on this page: <code>ls -a</code>, <code>cat</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>grep</code>, pipes, permissions, SSH keys.</li>\n<li>It's also good preparation for the Linux parts of your cybersecurity course.</li>\n<li>Keep a note of each level's password in your own file so you can resume.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "ssh bandit0@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220 # password for level 0: bandit0 each level's page tells you the goal and suggests commands to look up. it exercises everything on this page: ls -a , cat , find , grep , pipes, permissions, ssh keys. it's also good preparation for the linux parts of your cybersecurity course. keep a note of each level's password in your own file so you can resume."}]}, {"id": "term-scripting", "title": "Shell scripting basics", "blurb": "Saving commands in a file so you can run them again, with variables, conditions and loops.", "topics": [{"id": "script-first", "title": "Your first script", "src": "Fern", "idea": "A shell script is a text file of commands. Start it with a shebang line saying which shell runs it, make it executable with chmod +x, then run it with ./name.sh.", "deep": "<p><code>hello.sh</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"ch\">#!/bin/zsh</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># My first script</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hello, </span><span class=\"nv\">$USER</span><span class=\"s2\">!\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Today is </span><span class=\"k\">$(</span>date<span class=\"w\"> </span>+%A<span class=\"k\">)</span><span class=\"s2\">.\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"You are in </span><span class=\"k\">$(</span><span class=\"nb\">pwd</span><span class=\"k\">)</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>chmod<span class=\"w\"> </span>+x<span class=\"w\"> </span>hello.sh<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># once</span>\n./hello.sh<span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># run it</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><code>#!/bin/zsh</code> is the <strong>shebang</strong>. It tells the system which program runs the file. Use <code>#!/bin/bash</code> for scripts meant for Linux.</li>\n<li><code>#</code> starts a comment.</li>\n<li><strong>Why <code>./</code>?</strong> The current folder isn't in PATH, so you have to say \"the hello.sh right here\".</li>\n<li>You can also run it without <code>chmod</code>: <code>zsh hello.sh</code>.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "hello.sh : #!/bin/zsh # my first script echo \"hello, $user !\" echo \"today is $( date +%a ) .\" echo \"you are in $( pwd ) \" chmod +x hello.sh # once ./hello.sh # run it #!/bin/zsh is the shebang . it tells the system which program runs the file. use #!/bin/bash for scripts meant for linux. # starts a comment. why ./ ? the current folder isn't in path, so you have to say \"the hello.sh right here\". you can also run it without chmod : zsh hello.sh ."}, {"id": "script-variables", "title": "Variables and input", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Set a variable with name=value (no spaces), and use it with $name. Wrap variables in double quotes so values with spaces don't break.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"ch\">#!/bin/zsh</span>\n<span class=\"nv\">name</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"w\">                 </span><span class=\"c1\"># no spaces around =</span>\n<span class=\"nv\">course</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"CMSC 335\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hi </span><span class=\"nv\">$name</span><span class=\"s2\">, good luck in </span><span class=\"nv\">$course</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"nv\">today</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"k\">$(</span>date<span class=\"w\"> </span>+%F<span class=\"k\">)</span><span class=\"w\">           </span><span class=\"c1\"># $( ) runs a command and captures its output</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Backup for </span><span class=\"nv\">$today</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>-n<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"What topic are you studying? \"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">read</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>topic<span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"c1\"># read a line the user types</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Studying </span><span class=\"nv\">$topic</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quotes</th>\n<th>Behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>\"double\"</code></td>\n<td>variables expand: <code>\"Hi $name\"</code> → <code>Hi Noah</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'single'</code></td>\n<td>literal text: <code>'Hi $name'</code> → <code>Hi $name</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Math</strong> uses <code>$(( ))</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nv\">count</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"m\">3</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">$((</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">count</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">))</span><span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># 6</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Heads up:</strong> <code>name = \"Noah\"</code> (with spaces) fails. The shell thinks <code>name</code> is a command.</p>\n</blockquote>", "text": "#!/bin/zsh name = \"noah\" # no spaces around = course = \"cmsc 335\" echo \"hi $name , good luck in $course \" today = $( date +%f ) # $( ) runs a command and captures its output echo \"backup for $today \" echo -n \"what topic are you studying? \" read topic # read a line the user types echo \"studying $topic \" quotes behavior \"double\" variables expand: \"hi $name\" → hi noah 'single' literal text: 'hi $name' → hi $name math uses $(( )) : count = 3 echo $(( count * 2 )) # 6 heads up: name = \"noah\" (with spaces) fails. the shell thinks name is a command."}, {"id": "script-args", "title": "Script arguments", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Words you type after the script's name arrive as $1, $2 and so on. $# is how many there are and $@ is all of them.", "deep": "<p><code>greet.sh</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"ch\">#!/bin/zsh</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Script name: </span><span class=\"nv\">$0</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"First: </span><span class=\"nv\">$1</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Second: </span><span class=\"nv\">$2</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Count: </span><span class=\"nv\">$#</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"All: </span><span class=\"nv\">$@</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>./greet.sh<span class=\"w\"> </span>Java<span class=\"w\"> </span>Git\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>Script name: ./greet.sh\nFirst: Java\nSecond: Git\nCount: 2\nAll: Java Git\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p>Same idea as <code>String[] args</code> in Java, except numbering starts at <code>$1</code>.</p>", "text": "greet.sh : #!/bin/zsh echo \"script name: $0 \" echo \"first: $1 \" echo \"second: $2 \" echo \"count: $# \" echo \"all: $@ \" ./greet.sh java git script name: ./greet.sh first: java second: git count: 2 all: java git same idea as string[] args in java, except numbering starts at $1 ."}, {"id": "script-if", "title": "Conditions with if", "src": "Fern", "idea": "if [[ test ]]; then ... fi runs code only when the test is true. Tests can check whether a file exists, compare text, or compare numbers.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"ch\">#!/bin/zsh</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>-f<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]]</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Found Main.java\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">elif</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>-d<span class=\"w\"> </span>src<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]]</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"There's a src folder\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">else</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"No Java here\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">fi</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>True when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-f file</code></td>\n<td>file exists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-d folder</code></td>\n<td>folder exists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-z \"$var\"</code></td>\n<td>variable is empty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>\"$a\" == \"$b\"</code></td>\n<td>strings are equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>\"$a\" != \"$b\"</code></td>\n<td>strings differ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>$n -eq 5</code>, <code>-ne</code>, <code>-lt</code>, <code>-gt</code>, <code>-le</code>, <code>-ge</code></td>\n<td>number comparisons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>!</code></td>\n<td>not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code></td>\n<td>and, or</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Spaces inside <code>[[</code> and <code>]]</code> are required.</li>\n<li>The block ends with <code>fi</code> (if backwards).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exit codes:</strong> every command returns a number; <code>0</code> means success. <code>$?</code> holds the last one, and <code>exit 1</code> ends your script with a failure.</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>!<span class=\"w\"> </span>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java<span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Compile failed\"</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">exit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">fi</span>\n</code></pre></div>", "text": "#!/bin/zsh if [[ -f main.java ]] ; then echo \"found main.java\" elif [[ -d src ]] ; then echo \"there's a src folder\" else echo \"no java here\" fi test true when -f file file exists -d folder folder exists -z \"$var\" variable is empty \"$a\" == \"$b\" strings are equal \"$a\" != \"$b\" strings differ $n -eq 5 , -ne , -lt , -gt , -le , -ge number comparisons ! not && , || and, or spaces inside [[ and ]] are required. the block ends with fi (if backwards). exit codes: every command returns a number; 0 means success. $? holds the last one, and exit 1 ends your script with a failure. if ! javac main.java ; then echo \"compile failed\" exit 1 fi"}, {"id": "script-loops", "title": "Loops and functions", "src": "Fern", "idea": "for loops repeat over a list of items (like every .java file); while loops repeat as long as a test is true. Functions bundle commands under a name.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>file<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>*.java<span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Compiling </span><span class=\"nv\">$file</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"nv\">$file</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">done</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>i<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"m\">1</span>..5<span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Round </span><span class=\"nv\">$i</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">done</span>\n\n<span class=\"nv\">count</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"m\">3</span>\n<span class=\"k\">while</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$count</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>-gt<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]]</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"nv\">$count</span><span class=\"s2\">...\"</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nv\">count</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"k\">$((</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">count</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">))</span>\n<span class=\"k\">done</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Functions</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>greet<span class=\"o\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hello, </span><span class=\"nv\">$1</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n<span class=\"o\">}</span>\ngreet<span class=\"w\"> </span>Noah\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Loops end with <code>done</code>. Functions are called like commands, without parentheses.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "for file in *.java ; do echo \"compiling $file \" javac \" $file \" done for i in { 1 ..5 } ; do echo \"round $i \" done count = 3 while [[ $count -gt 0 ]] ; do echo \" $count ...\" count = $(( count - 1 )) done functions greet () { echo \"hello, $1 \" } greet noah loops end with done . functions are called like commands, without parentheses."}, {"id": "script-java-runner", "title": "Script project: a Java build-and-run script", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Put the compile-and-run steps for a project in one script, so ./run.sh rebuilds and runs it every time. This uses everything in this unit.", "deep": "<p><code>run.sh</code> (in your project folder, next to <code>src/</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"ch\">#!/bin/zsh</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># Compile everything in src/ into out/, then run Main.</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># Usage: ./run.sh [arguments for your program]</span>\n\nmkdir<span class=\"w\"> </span>-p<span class=\"w\"> </span>out\n\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Compiling...\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>!<span class=\"w\"> </span>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>-d<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>src/*.java<span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Compile failed. Fix the errors above.\"</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nb\">exit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">fi</span>\n\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Running...\"</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>-cp<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"nv\">$@</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>chmod<span class=\"w\"> </span>+x<span class=\"w\"> </span>run.sh\n./run.sh\n./run.sh<span class=\"w\"> </span>Noah<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">3</span><span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"c1\"># arguments pass through to main</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Extend it yourself:</strong> (hints, not answers)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Add a <code>clean</code> option: if <code>$1</code> is <code>\"clean\"</code>, delete <code>out/</code> and stop. <em>Hint: <code>if [[ ... ]]</code> and <code>rm -r</code>.</em></li>\n<li>Run the program with a test input file if one exists. <em>Hint: <code>-f</code> and <code>&lt;</code>.</em></li>\n<li>Time how long the run takes. <em>Hint: put <code>time</code> in front of a command.</em></li>\n</ul>", "text": "run.sh (in your project folder, next to src/ ): #!/bin/zsh # compile everything in src/ into out/, then run main. # usage: ./run.sh [arguments for your program] mkdir -p out echo \"compiling...\" if ! javac -d out src/*.java ; then echo \"compile failed. fix the errors above.\" exit 1 fi echo \"running...\" java -cp out main \" $@ \" chmod +x run.sh ./run.sh ./run.sh noah 3 # arguments pass through to main extend it yourself: (hints, not answers) add a clean option: if $1 is \"clean\" , delete out/ and stop. hint: if [[ ... ]] and rm -r . run the program with a test input file if one exists. hint: -f and < . time how long the run takes. hint: put time in front of a command."}]}, {"id": "term-next", "title": "Git and next steps", "blurb": "A first taste of Git, and drills to make the basics stick.", "topics": [{"id": "git-first", "title": "Your first Git commands", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Git tracks versions of your project from the terminal. These five commands are enough to start: init, status, add, commit and log. The Git page has the full guide.", "deep": "<p><strong>One-time setup</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>git<span class=\"w\"> </span>--version\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>config<span class=\"w\"> </span>--global<span class=\"w\"> </span>user.name<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah Hoagland\"</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>config<span class=\"w\"> </span>--global<span class=\"w\"> </span>user.email<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"your_email@example.com\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>The basic loop</strong></p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>~/projects/grade-analyzer\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>init<span class=\"w\">                     </span><span class=\"c1\"># start tracking this folder (once)</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>status<span class=\"w\">                   </span><span class=\"c1\"># what changed?</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>add<span class=\"w\"> </span>.<span class=\"w\">                    </span><span class=\"c1\"># stage all changes</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>commit<span class=\"w\"> </span>-m<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Add grade average method\"</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\"># save a snapshot</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>log<span class=\"w\"> </span>--oneline<span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"c1\"># history of snapshots</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><strong>Don't track compiled files:</strong> create a <code>.gitignore</code> file in the project:</p>\n<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>out/\n*.class\n.DS_Store\n.idea/\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><code>status</code> is the command to run constantly. It always tells you what to do next.</li>\n<li>Write commit messages that say what the change does.</li>\n</ul><p>Next: the <a href=\"#git\" data-subject=\"git\">Git page</a> covers remotes, pushing, tokens and GitHub Pages.</p>", "text": "one-time setup git --version git config --global user.name \"noah hoagland\" git config --global user.email \"your_email@example.com\" the basic loop cd ~/projects/grade-analyzer git init # start tracking this folder (once) git status # what changed? git add . # stage all changes git commit -m \"add grade average method\" # save a snapshot git log --oneline # history of snapshots don't track compiled files: create a .gitignore file in the project: out/ *.class .ds_store .idea/ status is the command to run constantly. it always tells you what to do next. write commit messages that say what the change does."}, {"id": "term-drills", "title": "Drills to make it stick", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Short exercises, each around 10 minutes, that use only the terminal. Do them in ~/terminal-practice so nothing important is at risk.", "deep": "<ol>\n<li><strong>Build a project skeleton</strong> (10 min): make <code>grade-analyzer/src</code> and <code>grade-analyzer/out</code> with one <code>mkdir -p</code>, create <code>Main.java</code> with <code>touch</code>, check with <code>tree</code> or <code>ls -R</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Write, compile, run</strong> (10 min): write Hello World in nano, compile with <code>javac -d out</code>, run with <code>java -cp out</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Test with input files</strong> (10 min): make a Scanner program, a <code>test.txt</code> input file, and run <code>java Main &lt; test.txt</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Find things</strong> (10 min): use <code>find</code> to list every <code>.java</code> file under <code>~/Desktop</code>, then <code>grep -rn \"Scanner\"</code> to see which ones read input.</li>\n<li><strong>Customize your shell</strong> (10 min): add two aliases to <code>~/.zshrc</code>, <code>source</code> it, and use them.</li>\n<li><strong>First script</strong> (15 min): write <code>run.sh</code> from the scripting unit and use it on drill 2's project.</li>\n<li><strong>First commit</strong> (10 min): <code>git init</code> the project, add a <code>.gitignore</code>, make your first commit.</li>\n<li><strong>SSH</strong> (open-ended): reach Bandit level 5.</li>\n</ol>", "text": "build a project skeleton (10 min): make grade-analyzer/src and grade-analyzer/out with one mkdir -p , create main.java with touch , check with tree or ls -r . write, compile, run (10 min): write hello world in nano, compile with javac -d out , run with java -cp out . test with input files (10 min): make a scanner program, a test.txt input file, and run java main < test.txt . find things (10 min): use find to list every .java file under ~/desktop , then grep -rn \"scanner\" to see which ones read input. customize your shell (10 min): add two aliases to ~/.zshrc , source it, and use them. first script (15 min): write run.sh from the scripting unit and use it on drill 2's project. first commit (10 min): git init the project, add a .gitignore , make your first commit. ssh (open-ended): reach bandit level 5."}]}], "ref": [{"id": "t-nav", "title": "Moving around", "html": "<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pwd</code></td>\n<td>where am I?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ls</code> / <code>ls -la</code> / <code>ls -lh</code></td>\n<td>list / all + details / readable sizes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cd folder</code></td>\n<td>go into a folder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cd ..</code> / <code>cd ../..</code></td>\n<td>up one / two levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cd</code> or <code>cd ~</code></td>\n<td>home</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cd -</code></td>\n<td>back to the previous folder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>open .</code></td>\n<td>open this folder in Finder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tree -L 2</code></td>\n<td>folder tree, 2 levels (after <code>brew install tree</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><code>/</code> root · <code>~</code> home · <code>.</code> here · <code>..</code> up one · quote names with spaces: <code>cd \"cs study\"</code></p>", "text": "command does pwd where am i? ls / ls -la / ls -lh list / all + details / readable sizes cd folder go into a folder cd .. / cd ../.. up one / two levels cd or cd ~ home cd - back to the previous folder open . open this folder in finder tree -l 2 folder tree, 2 levels (after brew install tree ) / root · ~ home · . here · .. up one · quote names with spaces: cd \"cs study\""}, {"id": "t-files", "title": "Files and folders", "html": "<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mkdir name</code> / <code>mkdir -p a/b/c</code></td>\n<td>make folder / nested folders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>touch file</code></td>\n<td>make an empty file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cp a b</code> / <code>cp -r dirA dirB</code></td>\n<td>copy file / folder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mv a b</code></td>\n<td>move or rename</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rm file</code> / <code>rm -r folder</code></td>\n<td>delete forever (no Trash)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rm -i file</code></td>\n<td>ask first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cat file</code></td>\n<td>print it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>less file</code></td>\n<td>scroll it (q quits)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>head -n 5 file</code> / <code>tail -n 5 file</code></td>\n<td>first / last 5 lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tail -f file</code></td>\n<td>watch new lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>wc -l file</code></td>\n<td>count lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nano file</code></td>\n<td>edit (Control-O save, Control-X exit)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>open -e file</code></td>\n<td>open in TextEdit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Wildcards: <code>*.java</code> = every file ending in .java</p>", "text": "command does mkdir name / mkdir -p a/b/c make folder / nested folders touch file make an empty file cp a b / cp -r dira dirb copy file / folder mv a b move or rename rm file / rm -r folder delete forever (no trash) rm -i file ask first cat file print it less file scroll it (q quits) head -n 5 file / tail -n 5 file first / last 5 lines tail -f file watch new lines wc -l file count lines nano file edit (control-o save, control-x exit) open -e file open in textedit wildcards: *.java = every file ending in .java"}, {"id": "t-pipes", "title": "Search, pipes and redirects", "html": "<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Syntax</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd &gt; file</code></td>\n<td>save output (replace)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd &gt;&gt; file</code></td>\n<td>save output (append)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd 2&gt; file</code></td>\n<td>save errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd &lt; file</code></td>\n<td>use file as input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd1 | cmd2</code></td>\n<td>pipe output into the next command</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd1 &amp;&amp; cmd2</code></td>\n<td>run cmd2 only if cmd1 succeeded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grep -rn \"text\" .</code></td>\n<td>find text in files (recursive, line numbers)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grep -i</code></td>\n<td>ignore case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>find . -name \"*.java\"</code></td>\n<td>find files by name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>history | grep word</code></td>\n<td>find a past command</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>diff a b</code></td>\n<td>show differences between files</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>", "text": "syntax does cmd > file save output (replace) cmd >> file save output (append) cmd 2> file save errors cmd < file use file as input cmd1 | cmd2 pipe output into the next command cmd1 && cmd2 run cmd2 only if cmd1 succeeded grep -rn \"text\" . find text in files (recursive, line numbers) grep -i ignore case find . -name \"*.java\" find files by name history | grep word find a past command diff a b show differences between files"}, {"id": "t-keys", "title": "Keyboard shortcuts", "html": "<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Keys</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab</td>\n<td>autocomplete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>↑ / ↓</td>\n<td>command history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control-C</td>\n<td>stop the running command</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control-L / <code>clear</code></td>\n<td>clear screen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control-A / Control-E</td>\n<td>start / end of line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control-U</td>\n<td>delete the line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control-R</td>\n<td>search history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>⌘-C / ⌘-V</td>\n<td>copy / paste</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>⌘-T</td>\n<td>new tab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>!!</code></td>\n<td>rerun the last command</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>", "text": "keys does tab autocomplete ↑ / ↓ command history control-c stop the running command control-l / clear clear screen control-a / control-e start / end of line control-u delete the line control-r search history ⌘-c / ⌘-v copy / paste ⌘-t new tab !! rerun the last command"}, {"id": "t-help", "title": "Help and info", "html": "<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>man cmd</code></td>\n<td>full manual (q quits, / searches)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cmd --help</code></td>\n<td>quick summary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tldr cmd</code></td>\n<td>short examples (after <code>brew install tldr</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>which cmd</code></td>\n<td>where the program lives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>echo $PATH</code></td>\n<td>where the shell looks for commands</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>history</code></td>\n<td>past commands</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>", "text": "command does man cmd full manual (q quits, / searches) cmd --help quick summary tldr cmd short examples (after brew install tldr ) which cmd where the program lives echo $path where the shell looks for commands history past commands"}, {"id": "t-java", "title": "Java from the terminal", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>java<span class=\"w\"> </span>-version<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;&amp;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>javac<span class=\"w\"> </span>-version\n/usr/libexec/java_home<span class=\"w\"> </span>-V<span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"c1\"># every installed JDK</span>\n\njavac<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;&amp;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>java<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\">               </span><span class=\"c1\"># compile, then run</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main.java<span class=\"w\">                             </span><span class=\"c1\"># single file, one step</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\"> </span>arg1<span class=\"w\"> </span>arg2<span class=\"w\">                        </span><span class=\"c1\"># pass arguments</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\"> </span>&lt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>input.txt<span class=\"w\"> </span>&gt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>output.txt<span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># input from / output to files</span>\n\njavac<span class=\"w\"> </span>-d<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>src/*.java<span class=\"w\">                    </span><span class=\"c1\"># compile into out/</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>-cp<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\">                          </span><span class=\"c1\"># run from out/</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>-cp<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>com.noah.app.Main<span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># class in a package</span>\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>-cp<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"out:lib/lib.jar\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"c1\"># with a library (: separator)</span>\n\njar<span class=\"w\"> </span>--create<span class=\"w\"> </span>--file<span class=\"w\"> </span>app.jar<span class=\"w\"> </span>--main-class<span class=\"w\"> </span>Main<span class=\"w\"> </span>-C<span class=\"w\"> </span>out<span class=\"w\"> </span>.\njava<span class=\"w\"> </span>-jar<span class=\"w\"> </span>app.jar\n\njavac<span class=\"w\"> </span>--module-path<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH_TO_FX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>--add-modules<span class=\"w\"> </span>javafx.controls<span class=\"w\"> </span>App.java\njava<span class=\"w\">  </span>--module-path<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH_TO_FX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>--add-modules<span class=\"w\"> </span>javafx.controls<span class=\"w\"> </span>App\n</code></pre></div>", "text": "java -version && javac -version /usr/libexec/java_home -v # every installed jdk javac main.java && java main # compile, then run java main.java # single file, one step java main arg1 arg2 # pass arguments java main < input.txt > output.txt # input from / output to files javac -d out src/*.java # compile into out/ java -cp out main # run from out/ java -cp out com.noah.app.main # class in a package java -cp \"out:lib/lib.jar\" main # with a library (: separator) jar --create --file app.jar --main-class main -c out . java -jar app.jar javac --module-path $path_to_fx --add-modules javafx.controls app.java java --module-path $path_to_fx --add-modules javafx.controls app"}, {"id": "t-brew", "title": "Homebrew", "html": "<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew search x</code> / <code>brew info x</code></td>\n<td>find / describe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew install x</code></td>\n<td>install a tool</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew install --cask x</code></td>\n<td>install a Mac app</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew list</code></td>\n<td>installed packages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew update &amp;&amp; brew upgrade</code></td>\n<td>update everything</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew outdated</code></td>\n<td>what has updates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew uninstall x</code></td>\n<td>remove</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew cleanup</code></td>\n<td>free space</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>brew doctor</code></td>\n<td>check for problems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>", "text": "command does brew search x / brew info x find / describe brew install x install a tool brew install --cask x install a mac app brew list installed packages brew update && brew upgrade update everything brew outdated what has updates brew uninstall x remove brew cleanup free space brew doctor check for problems"}, {"id": "t-setup", "title": "Shell setup", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>nano<span class=\"w\"> </span>~/.zshrc<span class=\"w\">                       </span><span class=\"c1\"># edit settings</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">source</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>~/.zshrc<span class=\"w\">                     </span><span class=\"c1\"># reload them</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">NAME</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"value\"</span><span class=\"w\">                 </span><span class=\"c1\"># environment variable</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">export</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">PATH</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"nv\">$HOME</span><span class=\"s2\">/bin:</span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># add a folder to PATH (keep :$PATH!)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">alias</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">ll</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"ls -lah\"</span><span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"c1\"># shortcut</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">alias</span><span class=\"w\">                               </span><span class=\"c1\"># list aliases</span>\nenv<span class=\"w\">                                 </span><span class=\"c1\"># list environment variables</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>chmod</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>chmod +x file</code></td>\n<td>make runnable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>chmod 755 file</code></td>\n<td>rwx r-x r-x (scripts)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>chmod 644 file</code></td>\n<td>rw- r-- r-- (normal files)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>chmod 600 file</code></td>\n<td>rw- --- --- (private keys)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>r = 4 · w = 2 · x = 1 · order: owner, group, others</p>", "text": "nano ~/.zshrc # edit settings source ~/.zshrc # reload them export name = \"value\" # environment variable export path = \" $home /bin: $path \" # add a folder to path (keep :$path!) alias ll = \"ls -lah\" # shortcut alias # list aliases env # list environment variables chmod result chmod +x file make runnable chmod 755 file rwx r-x r-x (scripts) chmod 644 file rw- r-- r-- (normal files) chmod 600 file rw- --- --- (private keys) r = 4 · w = 2 · x = 1 · order: owner, group, others"}, {"id": "t-ssh", "title": "SSH", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>ssh<span class=\"w\"> </span>user@host<span class=\"w\">                       </span><span class=\"c1\"># connect</span>\nssh<span class=\"w\"> </span>-p<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">2222</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>user@host<span class=\"w\">               </span><span class=\"c1\"># custom port</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">exit</span><span class=\"w\">                                </span><span class=\"c1\"># disconnect</span>\nssh-keygen<span class=\"w\"> </span>-t<span class=\"w\"> </span>ed25519<span class=\"w\"> </span>-C<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"email\"</span><span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\"># make a key pair</span>\nssh-copy-id<span class=\"w\"> </span>user@host<span class=\"w\">               </span><span class=\"c1\"># install your public key on a server</span>\nssh-add<span class=\"w\"> </span>--apple-use-keychain<span class=\"w\"> </span>~/.ssh/id_ed25519\npbcopy<span class=\"w\"> </span>&lt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># copy public key (for GitHub)</span>\nssh<span class=\"w\"> </span>-T<span class=\"w\"> </span>git@github.com<span class=\"w\">               </span><span class=\"c1\"># test GitHub</span>\nscp<span class=\"w\"> </span>file<span class=\"w\"> </span>host:~/<span class=\"w\">                    </span><span class=\"c1\"># copy to remote</span>\nscp<span class=\"w\"> </span>host:~/file<span class=\"w\"> </span>.<span class=\"w\">                   </span><span class=\"c1\"># copy from remote</span>\nscp<span class=\"w\"> </span>-r<span class=\"w\"> </span>folder<span class=\"w\"> </span>host:~/<span class=\"w\">               </span><span class=\"c1\"># copy a folder</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p>Private key <code>~/.ssh/id_ed25519</code> (never share) · public key <code>.pub</code> (share) · nicknames in <code>~/.ssh/config</code></p>", "text": "ssh user@host # connect ssh -p 2222 user@host # custom port exit # disconnect ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -c \"email\" # make a key pair ssh-copy-id user@host # install your public key on a server ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 pbcopy < ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub # copy public key (for github) ssh -t git@github.com # test github scp file host:~/ # copy to remote scp host:~/file . # copy from remote scp -r folder host:~/ # copy a folder private key ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 (never share) · public key .pub (share) · nicknames in ~/.ssh/config"}, {"id": "t-script", "title": "Shell scripting", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"ch\">#!/bin/zsh                     # shebang (#!/bin/bash on Linux)</span>\n<span class=\"nv\">name</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"w\">                    </span><span class=\"c1\"># variable: no spaces around =</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hi </span><span class=\"nv\">$name</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"w\">                </span><span class=\"c1\"># use it (double quotes)</span>\n<span class=\"nv\">now</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"k\">$(</span>date<span class=\"w\"> </span>+%F<span class=\"k\">)</span><span class=\"w\">                </span><span class=\"c1\"># capture command output</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">$((</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">))</span><span class=\"w\">              </span><span class=\"c1\"># math</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">read</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>answer<span class=\"w\">                    </span><span class=\"c1\"># user input</span>\n\n<span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"nv\">$1</span><span class=\"s2\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$2</span><span class=\"s2\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$#</span><span class=\"s2\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$@</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># arguments: first, second, count, all</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>-f<span class=\"w\"> </span>file<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]]</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>...<span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">elif</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>...<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]]</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>...<span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>...<span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fi</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>f<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>*.java<span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"nv\">$f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">done</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>i<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"m\">1</span>..5<span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>...<span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">done</span>\n<span class=\"k\">while</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nv\">$n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>-gt<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]]</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>...<span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">done</span>\n\ngreet<span class=\"o\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nb\">echo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hello, </span><span class=\"nv\">$1</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">exit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"w\">                         </span><span class=\"c1\"># end with failure ($? = last exit code)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p>Run: <code>chmod +x script.sh</code> then <code>./script.sh</code></p>", "text": "#!/bin/zsh # shebang (#!/bin/bash on linux) name = \"noah\" # variable: no spaces around = echo \"hi $name \" # use it (double quotes) now = $( date +%f ) # capture command output echo $(( 2 + 3 )) # math read answer # user input echo \" $1 $2 $# $@ \" # arguments: first, second, count, all if [[ -f file ]] ; then ... ; elif [[ ... ]] ; then ... ; else ... ; fi for f in *.java ; do echo \" $f \" ; done for i in { 1 ..5 } ; do ... ; done while [[ $n -gt 0 ]] ; do ... ; done greet () { echo \"hello, $1 \" ; } exit 1 # end with failure ($? = last exit code) run: chmod +x script.sh then ./script.sh"}, {"id": "t-git", "title": "First Git commands", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><span></span><code>git<span class=\"w\"> </span>config<span class=\"w\"> </span>--global<span class=\"w\"> </span>user.name<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Your Name\"</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>config<span class=\"w\"> </span>--global<span class=\"w\"> </span>user.email<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"you@example.com\"</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>init<span class=\"w\">                        </span><span class=\"c1\"># start tracking a folder</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>status<span class=\"w\">                      </span><span class=\"c1\"># what changed</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>add<span class=\"w\"> </span>.<span class=\"w\">                       </span><span class=\"c1\"># stage changes</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>commit<span class=\"w\"> </span>-m<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Message\"</span><span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># save a snapshot</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>log<span class=\"w\"> </span>--oneline<span class=\"w\">               </span><span class=\"c1\"># history</span>\ngit<span class=\"w\"> </span>clone<span class=\"w\"> </span>git@github.com:user/repo.git\n</code></pre></div>\n\n<p><code>.gitignore</code>: <code>out/</code> · <code>*.class</code> · <code>.DS_Store</code> · <code>.idea/</code></p>", "text": "git config --global user.name \"your name\" git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\" git init # start tracking a folder git status # what changed git add . # stage changes git commit -m \"message\" # save a snapshot git log --oneline # history git clone git@github.com:user/repo.git .gitignore : out/ · *.class · .ds_store · .idea/"}], "vocab": [{"title": "Basics", "terms": [["Terminal", "The Mac app that gives you a text window to type commands in."], ["Shell", "The program that reads your commands and runs them."], ["zsh", "The Z shell, macOS's default shell."], ["bash", "The Bourne Again Shell, the usual default on Linux. Very similar to zsh."], ["Command line (CLI)", "Controlling the computer by typing commands instead of clicking."], ["Prompt", "The text before your cursor that shows the shell is ready, ending in % on zsh."], ["Command", "The program to run, the first word you type."], ["Option (flag)", "A setting that changes how a command behaves, starting with - or --."], ["Argument", "What a command acts on, like a file or folder name."], ["man page", "A command's built-in manual, opened with man."], ["Tab completion", "Pressing Tab to finish a file or command name."], ["sudo", "Runs one command as an administrator. Use with care."]]}, {"title": "Files and paths", "terms": [["File system", "The tree of folders and files on a disk."], ["Directory", "Another word for folder."], ["Root", "The top of the file system, written /."], ["Home folder", "Your personal folder, /Users/yourname, written ~."], ["Working directory", "The folder you're in right now (pwd shows it)."], ["Path", "The route to a file or folder through the tree."], ["Absolute path", "A path starting from / or ~, which works from anywhere."], ["Relative path", "A path starting from the current folder."], ["Parent directory", "The folder one level up, written .."], ["Hidden file", "A file whose name starts with a dot, like .zshrc."], ["Wildcard", "A pattern like *.java that matches many names."], ["Recursive", "Including everything inside folders and their subfolders (-r)."]]}, {"title": "Pipes and output", "terms": [["Standard input (stdin)", "Where a program reads input from: the keyboard by default."], ["Standard output (stdout)", "Where a program writes normal output: the screen by default."], ["Standard error (stderr)", "Where a program writes error messages."], ["Redirect", "Sending output to a file (>) or input from a file (<)."], ["Pipe", "The | symbol, which feeds one command's output into another."], ["grep", "Searches for text inside files or output."], ["Exit code", "The number a command returns when it finishes. 0 means success."]]}, {"title": "Java tools", "terms": [["JDK", "Java Development Kit: the compiler, the java launcher and other tools."], ["javac", "The Java compiler. Turns .java files into .class bytecode."], ["java (launcher)", "Starts the JVM and runs a compiled class."], ["JAVA_HOME", "An environment variable naming which JDK to use."], ["Classpath (-cp)", "The list of folders and JARs where java looks for classes."], ["Output directory (-d)", "Where javac writes .class files."], ["JAR", "Java Archive. One file bundling compiled classes, runnable with java -jar."], ["Module path", "Where java finds modules like JavaFX (--module-path)."]]}, {"title": "Setup and Homebrew", "terms": [["Package manager", "A tool that installs, updates and removes software for you."], ["Homebrew", "The most common package manager for macOS."], ["Formula", "A Homebrew package for a command-line tool."], ["Cask", "A Homebrew package for a regular Mac app."], ["Environment variable", "A named setting the shell keeps, like HOME or PATH."], ["PATH", "The list of folders the shell searches for commands."], [".zshrc", "The settings file zsh runs for each new terminal window."], [".zprofile", "The settings file zsh runs at login. Homebrew's setup line goes here."], ["source", "Reloads a settings file in the current window."], ["Alias", "Your own short name for a longer command."], ["Permissions", "Read, write and execute rights for the owner, group and others."], ["chmod", "Changes a file's permissions."]]}, {"title": "SSH and scripting", "terms": [["SSH", "Secure Shell: an encrypted terminal session on another computer."], ["Host", "The remote computer you connect to."], ["Port", "A numbered entry point on a computer. SSH uses 22 by default."], ["Fingerprint", "A short code identifying a server's key, checked on first connection."], ["known_hosts", "The file where your Mac remembers servers it has connected to."], ["Key pair", "A private key you keep secret and a public key you share."], ["Passphrase", "A password protecting your private key."], ["ssh-agent / keychain", "Remembers your unlocked key so you don't retype the passphrase."], ["scp", "Secure copy: copies files over SSH."], ["Shell script", "A text file of shell commands that runs as a program."], ["Shebang", "The first line, #!/bin/zsh, saying which program runs the script."], ["Variable", "A named value in a script, set with name=value and used as $name."], ["Command substitution", "$(command), which runs a command and uses its output."], ["Positional parameters", "$1, $2 and so on: the arguments passed to a script."], ["Function", "A named group of commands inside a script."]]}], "res": [{"title": "Learn the basics", "items": [{"title": "The Missing Semester of Your CS Education (MIT)", "url": "https://missing.csail.mit.edu/", "note": "Free lectures and exercises on the shell, scripting, editors and Git. The best next step after this page."}, {"title": "Terminal User Guide (Apple)", "url": "https://support.apple.com/guide/terminal/welcome/mac", "note": "Apple's guide to the Terminal app itself: windows, profiles, settings."}, {"title": "explainshell", "url": "https://explainshell.com/", "note": "Paste any command and see what each part means."}, {"title": "tldr pages", "url": "https://tldr.sh/", "note": "Short, example-first help for common commands."}]}, {"title": "Tools", "items": [{"title": "Homebrew", "url": "https://brew.sh/", "note": "The install command and the official documentation."}, {"title": "Homebrew documentation", "url": "https://docs.brew.sh/", "note": "FAQs, troubleshooting and how formulae and casks work."}, {"title": "java launcher reference (Java 21)", "url": "https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/specs/man/java.html", "note": "Every option for the java command."}, {"title": "javac reference (Java 21)", "url": "https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/specs/man/javac.html", "note": "Every option for the compiler."}, {"title": "ShellCheck", "url": "https://www.shellcheck.net/", "note": "Paste a script and it points out bugs and risky lines."}]}, {"title": "SSH", "items": [{"title": "Connecting to GitHub with SSH", "url": "https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/connecting-to-github-with-ssh", "note": "GitHub's step-by-step guide to keys, the keychain and testing."}, {"title": "OverTheWire: Bandit", "url": "https://overthewire.org/wargames/bandit/", "note": "A free terminal game played over SSH. Great practice for your cybersecurity course."}]}], "files": [["No uploaded notes yet", "This page was written by Fern for zsh on your Mac, starting from the basics. Upload any terminal notes and they'll be merged in."]]};
